# Draft: winewayland.drv: Use layer-shell to render popup and context menus
# https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/11500 state=opened
# For all popup menus (class atom 32768) and custom popup menus, like those generated by electron, use wlr-layer-shell to render them. This allows the context menus to be placed where they are expected by the application, which is particularly important for context menus that are not owned by a surface (e.g., the context menu for a system tray icon).

Since Wayland prevents interception of mouse events not destined for the client, we need to piggyback on `keyboard_handle_leave` to figure out if the user has clicked away from the context menu, which we use as a signal to dismiss it.

Since context menus can be destroyed by a keypress (enter, esc, etc.) we need to explicitly send `release_all_keys` whenever a layer is destroyed to prevent phantom keystrokes. This has been generalised to any surface being destroyed, because I think that is actually more correct behaviour.

This also fixes a subtle layering issue in electron based popup-menus that causes them to render with an opaque background.

Since some compositors do not support layer-shell, when it is unavailable the D-Bus SNI integration for tray icons is fully disabled since the user experience of having an icon in the system tray but having the context menu appear _wherever the compositor feels like it_ is worse than the null driver floating system tray.
